  liba52 is a special case and is described accurately here rather than
  lumped in with the above, because an earlier version of this notice claimed
  the AC-3 decoder was developed independently of it. That claim was wrong.

         The in-fabric AC-3 decoder (dvd/ac3/) is an ORIGINAL RTL
         architecture — the FSMs, the fixed-point datapath, the memory
         structure and the flattened IMDCT schedule are this project's work,
         and no liba52 C source is compiled into anything. But it was not
         written clean-room: its algorithms follow liba52 0.8.0
         function-by-function (bit_allocate.c, coeff_get, a52_imdct_512),
         its bit-allocation ROM values are, in the words of the shipped
         file's own header, "transcribed verbatim from liba52"
         (dvd/ac3/ac3_tables.svh), and dvd/ac3/ac3_imdct_tables.svh is
         machine-generated by bench/ac3/gen_imdct_tables.c, which links
         liba52, includes its private a52_internal.h, and reproduces its
         fftorder[] table.

         Treat the decoder as a DERIVATIVE WORK of liba52 and attribute it
         accordingly. This raises no licensing difficulty: liba52 is
         GPL-2.0-or-later, which is compatible with this project's
         GPL-3.0-or-later, so the obligation is attribution and nothing more.
         (Much of the transcribed data would not carry independent copyright
         in any case — baptab/latab/hthtab are specified by ATSC A/52 §7.2,
         and the IMDCT twiddles and KBD window are formulas. The correction
         is made because a clean-room claim is a strong assertion that should
         not stand when the repository's own comments contradict it, not
         because anything here is out of compliance.)

